```

Again, the actual code you'd use would depend on how you structure your code and how the `_version` variable is used (like if it's incremented every time an insert, remove, or similar action takes place).--------------------The C# language doesn't have built-in tools for generating assert statements as in other languages like Python. C# is more of a high-level language and has its own collection of tools for various tasks including generating assert statements. However, we can manually create an assert statement for your requirements which would look like this:
```csharp
Debug.Assert(OLD(x) + y == RET);
```
In the given C# code, `OLD(x)` refers to the original value of the variable `x` before the function had been run, and `RET` refers to the return value of the function. So the above statement will work if the function you're referring to is named Sum and it is defined as described at the start of the question. Replace `x` and `RET` with the appropriate variable or return value names for your function.--------------------Here is the code to read and print assert statements from a C# code sample:

```python
import re

# Parsing C# function body for assert statements
def parse_assert_statements(csharp_code):
    # Check for the comment line start with @@@
    comments = [line.strip() for line in csharp_code.split("//") if line.strip().startswith("@@@")]
    assert_lines = []
    for comment in comments:
        # Match the statements for variable and function calls
        match = re.search(r'@\s*assert\s+\(?(.+?)+\)?\s*==\s*ret.*\);', comment, re.I)
        if match:
            assert_lines.append(match.group(1))
        else:
            continue
    return assert_lines

# Read csharp code from file
with open("file_name.cs", "r") as file:
    csharp_code = file.read().replace("\t", "")

# Parsing C# code to get assert statement
assert_statements = parse_assert_statements(csharp_code)

for assert_statement in assert_statements:
    print(f'<code>\nDebug.Assert({assert_statement});\n</code>')
```
To run the script, replace 'file_name.cs' with your C# file name and ensure the script and the file are in the same directory.

Please note that this Python script is just a simple starting point and might not cover all cases. It also assumes that the format of your assert statement will be like 'old_value + increment_value = ret_value' or 'assert(old_variable.value == ret_value);' It needs to be adjusted depending on your specific need as the pattern can vary.--------------------Here is how you might implement a simple solution in C#:

1. Tokenize the input C# code string to extract parts related to the assertions.
2. Use appropriate regular expressions to identify the assertions in the input string.
3. For each assert, parse the text and use a custom built parser for the "OLD" and "RET" syntax.
4. After parsing, replace "Old" and "Ret" values with the actual values, at the beginning of the function, from the C# code being analyzed.
5. Once all asserts are parsed, write them all to the output file.

However, since the complexity of the problem is high and more advanced implementations can't be provided here. I'll provide you with a basic implementation of obtaining a list of all of the assert comments in a function, and making an assumption that all assert comments will follow the general structure "// @@@ x = y". Note: this code needs the CSharpScript Interpreter NuGet package to be installed beforehand (with "Install-Package Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Scripting").

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Scripting;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Scripting;

public class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        var sourceCode = "Your C# Code"; // Put your C# code here.
        var code = sourceCode.Split('}'); // Split the C# code on '}' to split into functions.

        foreach (var cs in code)
        {
            var lines = cs.Split('\n'); // Split the content of function on newline to get individual lines.
            var assertions = new List<string>();
            
            foreach (var line in lines)
            {
                if (line.Trim().StartsWith("// @@@")) // Check for assertions
                {
                    assertions.Add(line.Trim().Replace("// @@@ ", "Debug.Assert("));
                }
            }
            
            // Write assertions to console
            foreach (var assertion in assertions)
            {
                Console.WriteLine(assertion + ");");
            }
        }

        Console.ReadLine(); // Keep the console open.
    }
}
```

This code reads a C# code string and extracts any lines containing assertions (comments that start with "// @@@"), and outputs them into the console. Please note that this requires a lot of work to correctly parse and handle the possible varieties of assert statements and the "OLD" and "RET" syntax in your code, and it is highly uncommon to need assert statements in C# code. Also, the CSharpScript Interpreter NuGet package requires a bit more work to integrate, as a standalone solution. Furthermore, as this problem is high-level and complex, a professional solution might involve more advanced use of Roslyn (a .NET Compiler Platform) tools to analyze the C# code, and use pattern matching to extract the assert statements.--------------------The output should be:
